Reviewer #1 — Narrative, policy framing, data transformation
Motivate the approach upfront. In the introduction, explicitly state the policy question (is the 3% inflation target appropriate, or would lower inflation reduce RPD?) and justify why the inflation–RPD relationship is a valid lens for assessing this. Acknowledge it's one approach among several, and note other reasons low/stable inflation is desirable besides minimizing relative price distortion.
Define RPD immediately at first mention. Give the concept — variability in relative prices across products — in plain language, ideally with a small worked example (e.g., two goods) rather than asserting it is abstract without illustrating it.
Address the mechanical inflation–RPD link. Since RPD is constructed from the same price series as headline inflation, consider whether it should be scaled/adjusted for the overall inflation level (e.g., comparing RPD at 30% vs. 3% headline inflation). Discuss how this affects interpretation — including the awkward implication that RPD rising below the 3% target could look like a policy problem.
Sharpen the inflation-target discussion. Clarify the history/status of the framework: was the 4.5% midpoint an informal understanding? Does the current 3% target allow any tolerance band, or is it a point target with no allowance?
Clarify what "optimal inflation" means in this paper. State explicitly whether the optimal-inflation discussion is based solely on minimizing relative price distortion, or whether it nets this against the known benefits of some positive inflation (e.g., downward nominal rigidity, effective lower bound considerations).
Reviewer #2 — Identification and bivariate framework limitations
Acknowledge the bivariate limitation of the rolling-window regressions. Add discussion that these estimates may conflate the inflation–RPD relationship with supply-side shocks (energy, food, exchange-rate pass-through) that drive both variables simultaneously.
Acknowledge the same limitation for the quantile-on-quantile (QQ) method. Note explicitly that QQ estimates are also bivariate and do not support a causal interpretation; omitted variables could be driving the observed quantile relationships.
Add an explicit identification/limitations discussion. State clearly that the causal interpretation used to motivate optimal inflation-target design is not established by these methods, and that confounders could bias results.
Consider extending to a multivariate framework, if feasible: a structural VAR or local-projections-with-controls specification that conditions on supply-side variables, to give a more defensible basis for the policy conclusions. (This is a "strongly encouraged" extension, not strictly mandatory — worth flagging to the user as a scope decision.)


Reviewer #3 — Econometric/methodological detail
Resolve the stationarity inconsistency in Table A2. 
ADF/PP fail to reject a unit root while KPSS rejects stationarity for both headline inflation and RPD, yet both are labeled "Stationary." Re-examine the tests, reconcile the conflicting results (e.g., discuss near-unit-root/fractional integration possibilities), and correct the classification and discussion accordingly.
Add a variable definition and data source table in Section 3, covering headline inflation, the 45 CPI components, RPD, and expected/unexpected inflation, with construction method and source for each.
Expand Table A1 (descriptive statistics) to include Jarque–Bera statistic, skewness, kurtosis, and a formal nonlinearity test (e.g., BDS test) for headline inflation and RPD.
Sharpen the Abstract and Introduction's contribution statement. 
Explicitly differentiate the paper from Ndou and Redford (2014) and Ndou and Gumata (2017) — specify what's new (sample period, semi-parametric/QQ methods, incorporation of 2017 and 2025 target changes).
Address the mechanical construction issue in RPD (Eq. 9). Since RPD is the CPI-weighted SD of the same 45 series aggregated into headline inflation (the regressor in Eqs. 10, 12, 17), part of the estimated relationship may be mechanical (a common shock to volatile categories like fuel/electricity raises both weighted mean and weighted variance simultaneously). Add a robustness check: re-estimate RPD excluding fuel and electricity, and/or decompose category contributions to RPD in high-inflation periods.
Address simultaneity/exogeneity of inflation. All models (Eqs. 10, 12, 17) treat inflation as exogenous. Discuss the risk of simultaneity bias given common shocks (exchange-rate pass-through, global commodity prices). 
Consider adding a local-projections exercise around dated exogenous shocks (2020 oil collapse, 2022 food/energy shock) or a Granger-causality test to support the causal framing used in the menu-cost/signal-extraction discussion.
Expand the conclusion's policy discussion and limitations. 
Separate the effect of the 2017 inflation-target change itself from the effect of concurrent large global shocks (COVID-19, energy shock, Russia–Ukraine war) before generalizing to other emerging markets. 
Add an explicit limitations paragraph and a paragraph on future research directions.
Trim Footnote 2 — it duplicates text already in the main body; shorten or remove.
Add standard errors or t-statistics to Table 1, alongside the significance stars.
Copyedit for exposition issues throughout (reviewer doesn't specify locations — likely need a full read-through pass).
Address statistical power in small subsamples. Pre-/post-2017 splits (~100 obs each) combined with quantile partitioning in the QQR exercise likely produce substantial uncertainty at the tails (τ→0, τ→1). The bootstrap is mentioned in a footnote but confidence bands are missing from Figures 5–8 — add pointwise confidence intervals to those figures and discuss power explicitly for the post-2017 subsample.